#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096354 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "e375ba82-b677-5e41-a81f-cea18470ab4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-405" :question/problem "For the function $y = -2x^3+2$ shown above, write down an expression for the area $\\mathcal{A}$ as a definite integral.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1086/Source/Images/0cc7c2b91797e6c24577ac66cfd8e215.png)" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x^3+2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1$.\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x^3+2\n$$\n\nbetween the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "cd5b3d07-472e-50cc-b584-569feb90f927" :question/math-academy-id "e-406" :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum." :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nthe function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4-x\n$$\n\nthe lower bound is $a=1$, and the upper bound is $b=3$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{3}(4 - x)dx &= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)f(x_{k})Δx \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[f(1 + k \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[f(1 + \\frac{2k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[(4 - (1 + \\frac{2k}{n})) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[(3 - \\frac{2k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096356 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "f7aaebcf-0a91-510e-8f09-513774affd6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-407" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b$, given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right]$." :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3$.\n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(x_k) = x_{k}^2\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^2\n$$\n\nand $b = 8$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096357 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "b21e2c30-fe7d-5f6b-ade2-7324be8d273a" :question/math-academy-id "e-408" :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x$ as the limit of a right Riemann sum." :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nthe function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nthe lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{2}x^{2}dx &= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)f(x_{k})Δx \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)[f(1 + k \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)[f(1 + \\frac{k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)[(1 + \\frac{k}{n})^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef93f509-5331-515f-b47a-33bd4cb6698c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270067} {:answer/id #uuid "35ff6de2-6bb5-5c2e-91e5-5eae28b6e04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270068} {:answer/id #uuid "8373bebd-a86a-5f63-961e-d51ca255c38d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270069} {:answer/id #uuid "b1aafa90-37ae-5417-9103-eb1dd5e1a987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270070} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2e98f8-a0ac-5736-9e15-234323df3532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35ff6de2-6bb5-5c2e-91e5-5eae28b6e04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270068} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df1ba1e-8cbd-517f-a31d-398fd756f396" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8ef22d3-07d6-525f-b1e0-653bfae49fd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113886" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4i}{n}-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e79b3575-a997-5836-bb6a-d292e7786211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270082} {:answer/id #uuid "0e669318-e415-5ea8-8fa1-db8e4a2def44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270083} {:answer/id #uuid "02754aa4-4318-5605-be44-c20100fc0858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270084} {:answer/id #uuid "41c15602-6dc2-5a9d-b818-b7626710b549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270085} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3f3e8f-7101-51e5-aae6-825fa79184eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41c15602-6dc2-5a9d-b818-b7626710b549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270085} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3c037ba-72fe-5e2b-b697-19ae648d831b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1804c8ea-5802-59e5-92d9-206715562863" :question/math-academy-id "q-113927" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a29d1477-1fbe-50a5-93ee-83bc7198733d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270092} {:answer/id #uuid "c23e851e-4fc8-5393-89ad-a1c2b6abd0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270093} {:answer/id #uuid "5ecabcbe-7d04-5005-abb8-376d380c4d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270094} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e8b526-3111-57c4-923d-6147df373d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270095} {:answer/id #uuid "c112f0ea-840e-5ad1-9a6c-943958ca2ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ecabcbe-7d04-5005-abb8-376d380c4d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270094} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c799a9-2412-52cf-9a9e-2b1fcce297df" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23c94ac6-51bf-5545-8513-402f0a143fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113941" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=4$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a41e59a-844e-5ba7-9bfa-84e0c7acc2a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{2}(x^{3} - 2x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095137} {:answer/id #uuid "d293c65e-1b66-5450-93e7-b85c8138a7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{1}^{1.5}(-x^{3} + 2x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095138} {:answer/id #uuid "e697f517-2f4c-5b29-a9c5-fa3dce350f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{1}^{0}(-x^{5} + x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095139} {:answer/id #uuid "eecfe745-8025-590d-9efd-b8cdd108e218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{1}(-x^{3} + 2x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095140} {:answer/id #uuid "555ca213-d2ad-5d55-a87d-a201c1fc24ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{2}^{0}(-x^{2} - 2x - 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eecfe745-8025-590d-9efd-b8cdd108e218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{1}(-x^{3} + 2x + 1)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095140} :answer-field/id #uuid "5944a245-4679-5c70-93ed-697eb82094b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd0dc7f3-b37b-5218-ae5c-70b1389fda98" :question/math-academy-id "q-30265" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1086/Source/Images/q-30265.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x) =-x^{3} + 2x + 1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80cede21-e3b5-501e-a9a9-d2e0c11bdda5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{3}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095143} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2d2966-63bc-55b8-b34a-b5bd972d5f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{0}^{3}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)^{2}\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095144} {:answer/id #uuid "357745ea-cdbe-5133-9e50-4d60041d798d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{-3}^{3}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095145} {:answer/id #uuid "40499ec4-d581-5cc6-bb6a-5eb07dd8b591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{-2}^{2}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095146} {:answer/id #uuid "5f899f7e-eed6-5869-b959-35854e8974aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{-1}^{1}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f899f7e-eed6-5869-b959-35854e8974aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle A = \\int_{-1}^{1}(x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3)\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095147} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bcf4f65-8908-5459-9f45-c028d98f3215" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095142}] :question/id #uuid "08ba21a9-5bfb-51e6-9985-bff89e68f2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-30273" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1086/Source/Images/q-30273.png)\n\nFor the function $y = f(x) = x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf514bb4-ecac-5d78-a01e-f1fa6b00f140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{3i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf514bb4-ecac-5d78-a01e-f1fa6b00f140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{3i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253122} :answer-field/id #uuid "82711246-7818-567e-acca-65c5baf0326d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ba8ef0d-4afc-57f3-a6e1-978f702b493c" :question/math-academy-id "q-315830" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=9{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9{(1+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d10c97e6-7ea4-5522-8feb-1d49481b5656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253125} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb83b21-9c07-50dc-ae3b-faa6b4be20c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb83b21-9c07-50dc-ae3b-faa6b4be20c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253126} :answer-field/id #uuid "f085c8ab-d5da-55c2-b14f-64548a810098" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aba5708e-7651-5cf7-ad88-1fb8cca73e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aba5708e-7651-5cf7-ad88-1fb8cca73e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253128} :answer-field/id #uuid "9749fad5-d5c5-57bf-af31-496187f79966" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "088d1080-7491-5e6a-87a2-5180d45dc341" :question/math-academy-id "q-346180" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={2}^{\\,{x}_{k}}+1.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}({2}^{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={2}^{x}+1\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333a7616-00e0-5dec-84c0-cf05d85dcdc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272395} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7d9368-f263-5b85-baea-121011f015f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272396} {:answer/id #uuid "b901cb74-5ff6-5c61-bec7-8f395c73b914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272397} {:answer/id #uuid "3b33a91b-a5a6-5c44-99b1-35296118308c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272398} {:answer/id #uuid "614ba837-f61a-56c4-b5c5-d85655d9d705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\left({lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b901cb74-5ff6-5c61-bec7-8f395c73b914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272397} :answer-field/id #uuid "5329d3a5-9f45-5ab5-a824-9e831f919a8b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ae0c14e-2353-57d2-951a-f4c503f5887d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49189" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+l\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{l}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2797070-4a7f-5613-9c10-77cd9fe785b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "f of x = $(\\sec^2 x)+2$; b = $\\pi/4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273387} {:answer/id #uuid "c638d26e-5f00-5ba1-8211-25afa62a6102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "f of x = $(\\sec^2 x)-1$; b = $\\pi/4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273388} {:answer/id #uuid "1a07f891-70d5-5cab-b0c1-f68ca985526c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "f of x = $\\sec^2 x$; b = $\\pi/4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273389} {:answer/id #uuid "a7574b3d-d37b-5097-a106-f45ebe1dec0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "f of x = $(\\sec^2 x)+1$; b = $\\pi/4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273390} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfe3496-a2ba-5551-bc38-a47500ef512a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "f of x = $(\\sec^2 x)-2$; b = $\\pi/4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a07f891-70d5-5cab-b0c1-f68ca985526c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "f of x = $\\sec^2 x$; b = $\\pi/4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273389} :answer-field/id #uuid "e95e5857-d54a-587c-8251-9746e505a89a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc6ae514-8e76-5ad4-a9b5-b333e0e65f07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73178" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}]}
